course: dictionary
lesson: 4805
title: Hummock: a bump in the land
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A hummock is a small, rounded bump. It can be a little hill of earth, a mound of grass, or a heap of snow. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it for clues. «Not another word, but scrape and scratch and dig and hunt round, especially on the sides of the hummocks.» The word hummock sits in the middle of digging and hunting, so it must be a place you can dig into, not something you can see across a room.
explain: Mole and Rat were lost in the snowy Wild Wood. They had to push over hummocks and through hollows before they found Badger's door. You can picture the land: lumpy, bumpy and hard to walk across. A story like this uses hummocks to make a place feel rough and wild, and a good place for animals to hide.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does «the sides of the small rounded mounds» still make sense? Yes, it does, so you have the right idea. When you are not sure, a dictionary will confirm it.
fact: A hummock is a small, rounded hill or mound of earth, grass or snow.
fact: A hummock can also be a ridge in a field of ice.
fact: «Hummocks» is the plural: one hummock, two hummocks.
fact: In the story, Mole and Rat struggle over hummocks and hollows in the snowy Wild Wood.
fact: Context clues, like digging and hunting around, can help you understand a new word before you look it up.
exercise: What is a hummock? | A flat, empty field with no bumps | *A small rounded hill or mound of earth, grass or snow | A river that has frozen solid | A deep hole dug in the ground
exercise: Mole and Rat crossed the snowy Wild Wood over hummocks. So the ground there was what? | Hot and dry | Smooth as a table | *Bumpy and lumpy | Full of water like a lake
exercise: «Scrape and dig on the sides of the hummocks.» Where were the animals digging? | On a flat road | Up in the sky | Under the sea | *On the sides of the bumps in the snow
test: A field of ice can have hummocks. What might a hummock in ice look like? | A hole melted through the ice | *A raised ridge or bump of ice | A puddle of water on the top | A flat frozen lake with no bumps
test: Which of these is most like a hummock? | A long straight road | A deep, fast river | A glass of cold water | *A small grassy mound on a hillside
exam: Which sentence uses hummock correctly? | The hummock was a loud noise in the kitchen | The hummock ran into the shop to buy bread | *The cat sat on a hummock of grass in the garden | We drank a hummock of orange juice
